    2  A   ablation_onset           2.502    85.10   8.234e+08  stylized
          Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on
          (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's
          light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~85 km as low-confidence.
          Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin,
          volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
    3  Y   first_yield             10.293    40.33   1.095e+12  physical
          Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 1.4 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and
          heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5
          / H_f in [R22]).
    4  F   flare                   10.492    39.22   1.975e+12  stylized
          Light rises x3.2 in 0.42 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with:
          yield gen 0 at 40.3 km (100% of m0). Dust groups supply 0% of the rise and 0% of the light
          at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and
          timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
    5  B   first_breakup           11.097    35.86   9.486e+12  stylized
          Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as
          dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of
          a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification.
          Light grew x8.7 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
